<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="680" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/policyholder-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">The 1943 Standard Fire Policy and Your Appraisal Rights</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · November 4, 2026</p><h1>The 1943 Standard Fire Policy and Your Appraisal Rights</h1><p class="lede">Most U.S. appraisal clauses were modeled on one New York statute. Here is what its standard fire policy appraisal clause says and how it plays out in a modern claim.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why a 1943 New York statute still matters</h2>
<p>If you have a homeowners or commercial property policy almost anywhere in the country, its appraisal wording probably descends from New York’s standard fire policy appraisal clause. The 1943 New York Standard Fire Policy, set out today in Insurance Law §3404, was widely adopted as a model, and its appraisal paragraph became the template insurers copied for decades. For New York policyholders, that means the rules you are reading are, in a sense, the original version.</p>
<p>Modern policies have been rewritten in plainer language and differ from one another, so always compare your own wording. But the structure below shows up again and again.</p>

<h2>The trigger: failure to agree</h2>
<p>The standard form allows appraisal when the insured and the company fail to agree on the actual cash value or the amount of loss. That trigger is about <strong>value</strong>. It does not invite the panel to decide whether a loss is covered, whether an exclusion applies, or whether a policy condition was breached.</p>
<p>In practice the line blurs. Whether hail damaged six roof slopes or two is often treated as an amount question; whether the damage was caused by hail at all may be argued as causation or coverage. How New York courts treat a given dispute depends on the facts, so if the carrier says an item is outside appraisal, consider talking to a New York attorney.</p>

<h2>The procedure, step by step</h2>
<p>The standard form lays out a short sequence that most modern clauses still follow:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written demand</strong> by either party</li>
<li><strong>Each side selects a competent and disinterested appraiser</strong> and notifies the other, within 20 days under the statutory form</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers select an umpire</strong>; if they fail to agree within 15 days, a judge of a court of record may appoint one on request</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers value the loss</strong>, stating separately actual cash value and loss</li>
<li><strong>Differences go to the umpire</strong>, and an award in writing by any two determines the amount</li>
<li><strong>Each side pays its own appraiser</strong> and shares the umpire and other expenses equally</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/appraisal-walkthrough/">appraisal walkthrough</a> explains how each of these steps tends to unfold in a real New York claim.</p>

<h2>“Competent and disinterested”</h2>
<p>These two words cause more friction than any others. Competent means the appraiser understands construction, estimating and the type of loss. Disinterested means the appraiser has no stake in the result beyond a fee. Courts in different places have reached different views on whether certain fee arrangements, such as a percentage of the award, compromise disinterest, and carriers sometimes raise the issue to challenge an appraiser.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Practical tip</b>Before hiring any appraiser, ask how they are paid, whether they have worked for the insurer or the contractor involved, and whether they will disclose that history to the other side. Get the fee arrangement in writing.</div>

<h2>What the clause leaves out</h2>
<p>The standard form is short, and many practical questions fall into its gaps:</p>
<h3>Deadlines</h3>
<p>The clause sets time frames for naming appraisers and an umpire, but generally not for finishing the appraisal. Meanwhile, the standard form also limits suits to two years after the loss, and many modern New York policies use a similar period. Do not assume appraisal pauses that clock; check your policy and ask an attorney.</p>
<h3>Depreciation and holdback</h3>
<p>Nothing in the clause tells the panel exactly how to present replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value. A careful award spells out all three by coverage part, which avoids arguments later about recoverable depreciation. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/acv-rcv-holdback-calculator/">ACV and RCV holdback calculator</a> shows how those figures interact.</p>
<h3>Coverage disputes</h3>
<p>The clause does not say what happens if the carrier believes part of the loss is not covered. Some awards list disputed items separately, so the number is known if coverage is later resolved. That approach should be discussed with both appraisers and, where needed, counsel.</p>

<h2>How New York regulation fits around it</h2>
<p>Appraisal sits inside a wider regulatory framework. The New York State Department of Financial Services oversees insurers, and Regulation 64 sets claim-handling standards such as timely acknowledgment and investigation. Insurance Law §2601 addresses unfair claim settlement practices, though private remedies under it are limited. None of that changes how an appraisal panel values a loss, but a DFS complaint is a separate avenue if you believe a claim is being handled improperly.</p>

<h2>Using the clause well</h2>
<p>The standard fire policy appraisal clause works best when the dispute is genuinely about numbers, the file is well documented and both appraisers engage in good faith. If you are weighing whether to invoke it, start with a clear comparison of the carrier’s estimate and your own, then <a href="/reach-us/">contact us</a> for a review before sending a demand.</p>
